@page "/Game"

<main>

    <div class="hero">
        <h1>Hex Strategy Game</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">A Civilization-inspired strategy game built from scratch in Unity.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
            <a href="https://github.com/fredo71" target="_blank" class="btn-styled">
                <i class="bi bi-github"></i> GitHub
            </a>
            <span class="wip-badge">Work in Progress</span>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* ───────────────────────── §1 — What is this? ───────────────────────── *@
    <section class="section">
        <div class="overview">
            <video class="game-screenshot"
                   src="video/hexmap-generation.mp4"
                   poster="video/hexmap-generation.jpg"
                   autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
                   aria-label="Hex terrain filling in region by region as the world is generated">
            </video>
            <div class="overview-text">
                <h2>What is this?</h2>
                <p>
                    <strong>A hexagonal world, procedurally generated, at a size you set.</strong>
                    Nothing is built before it is needed: the ground nearest you appears first, and the
                    rest fills in behind it while you are already moving. From a cold start the whole map
                    is finished in about four seconds.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="kpi-row">
            <StatTile Value="@TotalHexTiles" Label="hex tiles in the world" />
            <StatTile Value="@PctFramesBelow30Overview" Unit="%" Label="of frames below 30 fps" />
            <StatTile Value="@AvgFpsOverview" Unit="fps" Label="average across the whole run" />
            <StatTile Value="@TimeToFirstHex" Unit="s" Label="before the first hex is on screen" />
            <StatTile Value="@TreeCountWorld" Label="trees and foliage placed across the map" />
        </div>

        <div class="method">
            <h3>How this was measured</h3>
            <p>
                One 60-second capture. The game starts cold, generates the world, then flies a camera
                along a fixed waypoint path that repeats every 20 seconds so the same ground
                comes round a little over twice.
                <span class="rig">
                    Recorded on a GTX 1650 Ti laptop GPU with an i5-10300H, at 1920&times;1080
                    fullscreen, vsync off, no frame cap, from a development build in Unity 6000.5.7f1.
                </span>
            </p>

            <RunTimeline Title="The whole run, frame by frame"
                         Note="Every frame of the capture as frames per second. The shaded band spans the worst and best frame in each column, so no single spike is sampled away."
                         ElapsedS="RunTimelineElapsedS"
                         FpsPerFrame="RunTimelineFps"
                         Phases="RunTimelinePhases" />
        </div>
    </section>

    @* ───────────────────────── §2 — Building the world ───────────────────────── *@
    <section class="section">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Building the world</h2>
            <h3>What it costs to generate 225 chunks while the game keeps rendering.</h3>
        </div>

        <p class="section-note">
            Chunks are meshed on a pool of background worker threads, so the main thread barely notices
            225 of them building at once &mdash; frame rate holds steady while the world fills in.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@BuildTime" Unit="s" Label="to fill the whole map from cold" />
            <StatTile Value="@PctFramesBelow30Build" Unit="%" Label="of frames below 30 fps while building" />
        </div>

        <div class="chart-grid">
            <LineChart Title="Hexes finished over time"
                       Note="Cumulative hex tiles finished, from a cold start to the whole map."
                       Values="HexesFinishedOverTime"
                       Unit="hexes"
                       XAxisLabel="second"
                       XAxisLabelPlural="seconds"
                       XTitle="seconds since generation started" />
            <LineChart Title="Frame rate while building"
                       Note="Frames per second across the same window."
                       Values="FpsOverTimeBuild"
                       Unit="fps"
                       XAxisLabel="second"
                       XAxisLabelPlural="seconds"
                       Thresholds="FpsThresholds" />
        </div>

        <p class="section-note">
            Memory for the map is reserved once, up front, so nothing grows while chunks are still
            being built.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@AvgRamPctBudget" Unit="%" Label="of the memory budget, on average while building" />
            <StatTile Value="@MaxRamPctBudget" Unit="%" Label="of the memory budget, at peak while building" />
        </div>

        <LineChart Title="Memory (RAM) used while building"
                   Note="Percentage of the stated memory budget, sampled every frame."
                   Values="RamPctOverTime"
                   Unit="%"
                   XAxisLabel="second"
                   XAxisLabelPlural="seconds" />
    </section>

    @* ───────────────────────── §3 — Once it is built ───────────────────────── *@
    <section class="section">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Once it is built</h2>
            <h3>49 seconds of flying over the finished map.</h3>
        </div>

        <p class="section-note">
            Every tile on the map casts a shadow, not just the ones near the camera &mdash; but what
            actually matters is how many of those shadows are live on screen at once.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@ShadowsOnScreen" Label="hexes casting a live shadow on screen at once" />
        </div>

        <video class="asset-media"
               src="video/hexmap-shadows.mp4"
               poster="video/hexmap-shadows.jpg"
               autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata"
               aria-label="Shadows sweeping across the finished map, camera fixed">
        </video>

        <p class="section-note">
            Detail drops off gradually with distance instead of popping in, so nothing changes abruptly
            as it leaves view.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@DrawDistanceHexes" Label="hexes visible before elements are culled" />
            <StatTile Value="@BiggestTrianglePctOfScreen" Unit="%" Label="of the screen &mdash; the largest triangle, worst case" />
        </div>

        <img class="asset-media"
             src="images/hexmap-triangles.png"
             alt="Finished terrain with each triangle rendered a distinct flat color" />

        <p class="section-note">
            All of that stays inside the video-memory budget, even with the whole map&rsquo;s shadows and
            detail live at once.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@MaxGpuPctBudget" Unit="%" Label="of the video-memory budget, at peak" />
            <StatTile Value="@AvgGpuPctBudget" Unit="%" Label="of the video-memory budget, on average" />
        </div>

        <LineChart Title="GPU memory used"
                   Note="Percentage of the video-memory budget, sampled every frame."
                   Values="GpuOverTime"
                   Unit="%"
                   XAxisLabel="second"
                   XAxisLabelPlural="seconds" />

        <p class="section-note">
            None of it costs frame rate &mdash; the map holds comfortably above 30 fps once everything
            is built.
        </p>

        <div class="kpi-row kpi-row--compact">
            <StatTile Value="@AvgFpsBuilt" Unit="fps" Label="average frame rate, whole map drawn" />
        </div>

        <LineChart Title="Frame rate, once the map is built"
                   Note="Frames per second across the 49-second flythrough."
                   Values="FpsOverTimeBuilt"
                   Unit="fps"
                   XAxisLabel="second"
                   XAxisLabelPlural="seconds"
                   Thresholds="FpsThresholds" />
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="section-head">
            <h2>Under the hood</h2>
            <h3>The systems behind the game.</h3>
        </div>

        <div class="feature-cards">
            <div class="feature-card">
                <h3>Coordinate System</h3>
                <p>
                    Each tile has a position on a <span class="code-inline">2D offset grid</span> that maps
                    directly to a location in the world. Finding the six neighbors of any tile is done by
                    checking whether the tile is on an even or odd row, which shifts the neighboring columns
                    by half a step. To convert a click in the world back to a tile, the position is projected
                    into <span class="code-inline">cube coordinate space</span> where the three axes always
                    sum to zero, then rounded to the nearest valid tile.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-card">
                <h3>Burst Job System</h3>
                <p>
                    Building the map mesh happens entirely off the main thread so the game never freezes
                    during generation. The main thread hands each chunk to a
                    <span class="code-inline">thread pool</span> of workers, each of which launches three
                    <span class="code-inline">Burst-compiled</span> Unity Jobs in parallel: one computes
                    vertex positions, one texture coordinates, and one lighting normals. Mesh data lives in
                    <span class="code-inline">unmanaged NativeArrays</span> rather than on the managed heap.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="feature-card">
                <h3>Decorator System</h3>
                <p>
                    Trees and foliage are rendered using <span class="code-inline">GPU instancing</span> via
                    Unity&rsquo;s <span class="code-inline">DrawMeshInstanced</span>, sending thousands of
                    positions to the GPU in a single draw call without creating any objects in the scene.
                    Close to the camera, full 3D meshes are used. Further away they switch to flat
                    <span class="code-inline">impostor billboards</span> that look similar but are far
                    cheaper to render. As the camera approaches, more instances gradually appear by
                    <span class="code-inline">density band</span>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

</main>
